WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.182
Most of the time, we will not type our data directly into Python.

00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:06.752
We will load it from somewhere.

00:00:06.752 --> 00:00:14.257
One of the most common formats is CSV, which stands for comma-separated values.

00:00:14.257 --> 00:00:20.892
In this example, we create CSV text inside Python and then use StringIO so Pandas can treat that

00:00:20.892 --> 00:00:22.727
text like a small file.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:27.137
The important function is pd.read_csv.

00:00:27.137 --> 00:00:31.337
Click Run. Pandas reads the header row and uses those values as column names.

00:00:31.337 --> 00:00:35.842
Each later line becomes one row in the DataFrame.

00:00:35.842 --> 00:00:43.867
We also print the type of the result, and you should see that it is a Pandas DataFrame.

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:49.717
In real work, the syntax may be as simple as pd.read_csv followed by the filename.

00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:55.362
The same general idea applies when data comes from SQL.

00:00:55.362 --> 00:01:01.062
SQL may retrieve the rows, and Pandas becomes the environment where we inspect, clean, transform,

00:01:01.062 --> 00:01:09.082
and summarize those rows. A good practice is to never assume that a file loaded perfectly.

00:01:09.082 --> 00:01:11.562
Loading is only the first step.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:14.038
The next step should always be inspection.
